Plugin code: MIT License (see LICENSE).

The plugin's references-only package ships names of liturgical days and
celebrations, ranks and colours, and Scripture and patristic citations: no
Scripture text, no liturgical prose, no saint biographies and no commentary
excerpts. The "-embedded-content" package adds the embedded texts and pictures listed at
the end (texts/), each under its own terms. It is not an official liturgical
book.

Calendar data
- General Roman and national calendar facts and celebration names are derived
  from Romcal (https://github.com/romcal/romcal), copyright its contributors,
  MIT License.

Mass reading references (citations only)
- United States: derived from the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops
  liturgical calendar and daily readings pages (https://bible.usccb.org/).
  Only factual assignments and Scripture citations are used.
- General Roman calendar: derived from LiturgicalCalendarAPI
  (https://github.com/Liturgical-Calendar/LiturgicalCalendarAPI), copyright its
  contributors, Apache License 2.0. Only citation assignments are used.
- Italy: derived from Parola Viva open data (https://parolaviva.art/opendata),
  curated by Giovanni Novelli, CC BY 4.0. Only citation assignments are used.
- Other calendars reuse these citation sets where the celebration is the same
  and are marked as computed, not verified.

Office of Readings references
- Author and work references are derived from the Office of Readings index at
  https://church.catholic-resources.org/ (facts only; no reading text).

Pre-1970 Missal editions (1570 to 1962)
- The calendars, ranks, colours, titles and Scripture citations of the older
  Roman Missal editions are derived from Divinum Officium
  (https://github.com/DivinumOfficium/divinum-officium), Copyright (c) 2026
  Divinum Officium, MIT License. Only these facts are used; no liturgical text.

Roman Martyrology references
- The entries of the Roman Martyrology (editio altera 2004) by day, with their
  names in Latin, English and Italian (source/martyrology/), are from the
  Common Roman Martyrology Eulogy Data Repository (CRMEDR,
  https://github.com/CatholicOS/crmedr), Catholic Digital Commons Foundation,
  licensed CC BY-NC-ND 4.0, used unchanged.

Gospel commentary references
- Catena Aurea author attributions are derived from the structured English
  Catena at https://github.com/AlvaroBalbin/catena (attribution names only).

Embedded texts and pictures (texts/, in the "-embedded-content" package only)
- Bible (English): adapted from the World English Bible (Catholic), eBible.org
  (https://ebible.org/), public domain.
- Bible (Latin): Biblia Sacra Vulgatae editionis (Clementine Vulgate, 1592),
  eBible.org, public domain (not the Nova Vulgata of today's Lectionarium).
- Bible (French): adapted from the Sainte Bible néo-Crampon Libre, © 2022
  Fraternité de Tibériade, CC BY-SA 4.0 (eBible.org): "le Seigneur" is read
  for "Yahweh", as in the liturgy. The adaptation stays under CC BY-SA 4.0.
- Bible (German): Die Heilige Schrift des Alten und Neuen Testamentes,
  übersetzt von Joseph Franz von Allioli, durchgesehen von Augustin Arndt SJ
  (Regensburg 1914), public domain, in the digital text of
  https://github.com/AlexBocken/allioli (Unlicense); Romans, Hebrews, 1-2 Kings,
  Ezra, Malachi 4, Zechariah 14, Esther's additions and the end of Psalm 9,
  which that text lacks, from the same Bible's PDF (archive.org, "Lord Henfield
  Edition 2025"): only the Arndt verses are taken.
- Bible (Italian): La Sacra Bibbia, tradotta in lingua italiana da Antonio
  Martini (1769-1781), public domain, in the digital text of Parola Viva's open
  data (CC BY 4.0) with corrections from Targum. A language of the 18th
  century; the psalms are re-cut at the Vulgate's verses.
- Bible (Portuguese): adapted from the Bíblia Portuguesa Mundial, eBible.org,
  public domain (a translation still under revision); the Name and "God",
  printed in capitals, are in lower case.
- Bible (Spanish): adapted from the Santa Biblia libre para el mundo,
  eBible.org, public domain: "el Señor" for "Yahvé", as in the liturgy.
- Bible (Russian): the Synodal translation (1876), eBible.org, public
  domain; without the deuterocanonical books.
- Bible (Chinese): the Chinese Union Version with new punctuation (1919/1989),
  eBible.org, public domain, in simplified and traditional script; a
  Protestant translation without the deuterocanonical books.
- Bible (Polish): the Wujek Bible (Jakub Wujek, 1599; the 1923 edition, in
  19th-century spelling), public domain, from the Wikisource transcription
  (CC BY-SA 4.0, https://pl.wikisource.org/wiki/Biblia_Wujka_(1923)); without
  the deuterocanonical books.
- Bible supplements (texts/bible/*/supplements.lua): containing passages and
  numbering corrections reuse the credited edition. Missing deuterocanonical
  passages in Polish, Russian and Chinese are supplemental AI translations
  from the public-domain English Bible by OpenAI Codex GPT-6.1 Sol (medium
  reasoning). Their separate attribution identifies the source and model;
  they are not wording from the Wujek, Synodal or Chinese Union editions.
- Rosary prayers and the Litany of Loreto: public-domain prayer books (A
  Manual of Prayers, Baltimore 1889; Catéchisme illustré, Paris 1906;
  Comtesse de Ségur, 1858; É.-M. Faillon, 1860; El alma con Dios, 1902;
  Marien-Büchlein, 1895; the Catecismo do Patriarcado for the Portuguese Hail
  Mary), some transcribed on Wikisource (CC BY-SA). What they lack is
  translated from the traditional Latin by the AI model Claude Opus 5.5 and
  marked with a star (*). Latin: traditional texts, public domain. The
  Polish, Russian and Chinese prayers and Litany are translated directly
  from Latin by OpenAI Codex GPT-6.1 Sol (medium reasoning), also marked *.
  Each language file states its source and actual model. The
  mysteries' short titles follow vatican.va's "The Holy Rosary".
- Catena Aurea texts (texts/catena/, English): the Oxford translation edited
  by John Henry Newman (1841-1845), public domain, from the structured Catena
  (https://github.com/AlvaroBalbin/catena, MIT).
- Office of Readings texts (texts/office/, English): older translations of the
  Fathers' works, public domain: Ante-Nicene
  Fathers and Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (1885-1899), the Ante-Nicene
  Christian Library (1870), and the Oxford translation of Chrysostom, as named
  in each text's own edition line (transcriptions by Wikisource, the
  Christian Classics Ethereal Library, tertullian.org and Project Gutenberg). The chapters that contain the Office's
  reading are given, not the Office's own (ICEL) wording. Readings of Latin
  Fathers with no free English translation are the Latin of Migne's
  Patrologia Latina (Paris, 1844-1865, public domain, via the Internet
  Archive's edition) translated into English by the AI model Claude Opus 5.5;
  newer additions also use OpenAI Codex GPT-6.1 Sol. Each such reading names
  the model that actually translated it in its own source line.
- Roman Martyrology texts (texts/martyrology/): The Roman Martyrology
  (Baltimore, 1916) and Martyrologium Romanum (Mechelen, 1913), public
  domain, as aligned by CatholicOS/martyrology-api (Apache-2.0); their
  translations into other languages by the AI model Claude Opus 5.5 (marked
  *); names from Wikidata (CC0); article leads from Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0),
  each credited with its article.
- Martyrology fallback descriptions preserve existing local text and fill
  individual missing entries from the English 1916 or Latin 1913 public-domain
  texts, then English Wikipedia where no historical text is available.
  Translations are by OpenAI Codex GPT-6.1 Sol (medium reasoning), marked *,
  with source, language and model attributed per entry. Traditional Chinese
  fallback translations may be converted from Simplified Chinese with
  OpenCC, as stated in their attribution. Wikipedia adaptations remain
  CC BY-SA 4.0 and retain a link to the original English article.
- Pictures (texts/pictures/): images from Wikimedia Commons
  (https://commons.wikimedia.org/), reduced and converted to greyscale. Each
  keeps its own licence (public domain, CC0, CC BY or CC BY-SA), named with its
  author in the credit shown under it and in index.lua; the CC BY-SA pictures
  remain under CC BY-SA. Every source file is listed in the repository's
  sources/manifests/saint-pictures-commons.json.
